The consensus firmly establishes $n_1 = 6$ and $n_2 = 10$ from the Picard-Fuchs order condition $n \equiv 2 \pmod 4$, setting the argument to $\alpha = (n_1 - 1)/n_2 = 1/2$. The resulting cubic Hamiltonian $H(p, q) = \frac{1}{2}(p^2 + q^2 - \frac{2}{3\sqrt{3}}q^3)$ yields the classical hypergeometric period function $T(\alpha) = 2\pi \,_2F_1(1/6, 5/6; 1; \alpha)$ normalized by $T(0) = 2\pi$. Applying Gauss's second summation theorem at $\alpha = 1/2$ directly gives the exact value $T(1/2) = \frac{2\pi^{3/2}}{\Gamma(7/12)\Gamma(11/12)}$.
